    <span>Last Updated: <strong>July 30, 2026</strong></span>
    <span class="separator" aria-hidden="true">•</span>
    <span>Editorially Reviewed by <a href="/author/jackie-bruce/"><strong>Jackie Bruce</strong></a></span>
    <span class="separator" aria-hidden="true">•</span>
    <span>Data Source: <a href="https://www.nplindia.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>CSIR-NPL National Physical Laboratory India</strong></a></span>
  </div>

  <div class="article-wrapper">
    <!-- Top Answer Summary Box -->
    <div class="summary-box speakable-summary">
      <div class="summary-header">
        <span class="summary-badge">TIME ZONE PROFILE</span>
        <h2>India Standard Time (IST) Summary</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="summary-grid">
        <div class="summary-item">
          <span class="label">UTC Offset:</span>
          <span class="val highlight">UTC+5:30 (5.5 hours ahead of GMT)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="summary-item">
          <span class="label">Reference Meridian:</span>
          <span class="val">82.5° East (Mirzapur / Prayagraj)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="summary-item">
          <span class="label">DST Observance:</span>
          <span class="val">None (Fixed year-round standard)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="summary-item">
          <span class="label">Primary Regions:</span>
          <span class="val"><a href="/india-time-zone/">India</a>, Sri Lanka (1.4+ Billion Population)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="summary-item">
          <span class="label">Major Cities:</span>
          <span class="val"><a href="/delhi-time/">Delhi</a>, <a href="/mumbai-time/">Mumbai</a>, Bengaluru, Hyderabad, Chennai</span>
        </div>
        <div class="summary-item">
          <span class="label">Time Diff to EST:</span>
          <span class="val">IST is 10.5 hours ahead of <a href="/est/">EST (UTC-5)</a></span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="summary-text">
        <strong>India Standard Time (IST)</strong> is the official standard time observed across the Republic of India and Sri Lanka. Operating at a fixed 30-minute fractional offset of <strong>UTC+5:30</strong>, IST governs 1.4 billion people under a single national time zone. It is 5 hours and 30 minutes ahead of <a href="/gmt/">Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)</a> and does not observe Daylight Saving Time.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <nav class="about-toc" aria-label="Table of Contents">
      <h2>Table of Contents</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#what-is-ist">1. What is India Standard Time (IST)?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#half-hour-offset">2. The Mathematics Behind the UTC+5:30 Half-Hour Offset</a></li>
        <li><a href="#acronym-disambiguation">3. Disambiguation: India vs. Irish vs. Israel Standard Time</a></li>
        <li><a href="#conversion-tables">4. Global IST Time Conversion Tables (GMT, EST, PST, CET)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#outsourcing-and-tech">5. Global IT Outsourcing & Follow-the-Sun Operations</a></li>
        <li><a href="#dual-timezone-debate">6. The Debate Over Two Timezones in India</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faqs">7. Frequently Asked Questions (20 Detailed Answers)</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <!-- Content Sections -->
    <section id="what-is-ist" class="content-section">
      <h2>1. What is India Standard Time (IST)?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>India Standard Time (IST)</strong> was established as the national standard time of India on September 1, 1906. Positioned at <strong>UTC+5:30</strong>, IST is calculated from the observatory tower in Mirzapur (near Prayagraj, Uttar Pradesh), which lies precisely on the <strong>82.5° East longitude meridian</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Prior to 1906, major Indian cities maintained separate local time standards—most notably Bombay Time (UTC+4:51) and Calcutta Time (UTC+5:53). Today, IST serves as the single unified time zone for the entire Indian subcontinent.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- SVG Diagram 1: 82.5 East Meridian -->
    <section class="diagram-section">
      <div class="diagram-card">
        <h3>Figure 1: India 82.5° East Longitude Reference Meridian</h3>
        <div class="svg-wrapper">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 800 240" width="100%" height="auto" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="India 82.5 East Meridian Diagram">
            <rect width="800" height="240" fill="var(--panel)" rx="12" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>
            
            <!-- Prime Meridian 0 -->
            <line x1="120" y1="40" x2="120" y2="200" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-dasharray="4,4"/>
            <text x="120" y="30" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--muted)" font-size="12">0° Prime Meridian (GMT)</text>

            <!-- Arrow East -->
            <path d="M 140 120 L 440 120" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/>
            <text x="290" y="105" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="12" font-weight="bold">+82.5° East Longitude (+5h 30m)</text>

            <!-- 82.5 East Meridian Line -->
            <line x1="460" y1="30" x2="460" y2="210" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"/>
            <text x="460" y="25" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--accent)" font-family="Space Grotesk, sans-serif" font-weight="bold" font-size="14">82.5° E Meridian (IST)</text>

            <!-- City Node -->
            <circle cx="460" cy="120" r="28" fill="var(--accent)" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width="2"/>
            <text x="460" y="125" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-weight="bold" font-size="13">Mirzapur</text>
            <text x="460" y="165" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11">Prayagraj / Uttar Pradesh</text>

            <!-- Easternmost Boundary -->
            <line x1="700" y1="40" x2="700" y2="200" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-dasharray="4,4"/>
            <text x="700" y="30" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--muted)" font-size="12">97° E (Arunachal Pradesh)</text>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <p class="diagram-caption">
          Figure 1: Illustration showing the 82.5° East longitude meridian passing through Mirzapur, establishing the UTC+5:30 baseline for IST.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="half-hour-offset" class="content-section">
      <h2>2. The Mathematics Behind the UTC+5:30 Half-Hour Offset</h2>
      <p>
        Most global time zones advance in full one-hour increments from UTC. However, India selected a 30-minute fractional offset (UTC+5:30) to create a single national time standard that perfectly balances solar noon between its westernmost point in Gujarat (68° E) and easternmost point in Arunachal Pradesh (97° E).
      </p>
      <p>
        Mathematically, each degree of longitude represents 4 minutes of solar time difference (82.5° × 4 mins = 330 mins = 5.5 hours).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="acronym-disambiguation" class="content-section">
      <h2>3. Disambiguation: India vs. Irish vs. Israel Standard Time</h2>
      <p>
        The abbreviation <strong>IST</strong> is shared by three distinct international time zones:
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Time Zone Name</th>
              <th>Abbreviation</th>
              <th>UTC Offset</th>
              <th>Primary Region</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>India Standard Time</strong></td>
              <td>IST</td>
              <td><a href="/utc/">UTC+5:30</a></td>
              <td>India, Sri Lanka (Year-Round)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Irish Standard Time</strong></td>
              <td>IST</td>
              <td><a href="/utc/">UTC+1</a></td>
              <td>Republic of Ireland (Summer Daylight Time)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Israel Standard Time</strong></td>
              <td>IST</td>
              <td><a href="/utc/">UTC+2</a></td>
              <td>Israel (Winter Standard Time)</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="conversion-tables" class="content-section">
      <h2>4. Global IST Time Conversion Tables (GMT, EST, PST, CET)</h2>
      <p>
        Convert India Standard Time (IST, UTC+5:30) to major global time zones using the reference table below:
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>IST (UTC+5:30)</th>
              <th>GMT (UTC+0)<br/><a href="/gmt-to-ist/">IST to GMT</a></th>
